WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:01.680
- This is liquid helium.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:05.520
It's currently sitting at
negative 269 degrees Celsius.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.480
That's just four degrees
above absolute zero.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.040
But watch what happens if
you start cooling it down.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.320
It starts to boil violently.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:20.000
- That's aggressive.
- There are bubbles everywhere.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.520
That is until 2.17 Kelvin.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.680
At that point, something strange happens.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.080
The helium has transformed
into a superfluid.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.800
- Ooh. It's completely still again.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.080
- What we've got is a beaker
with a leak tight aerogel base.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.840
- This beaker holds
the liquid helium well,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.680
but the moment it turns superfluid, it starts to leak

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:50.280
through.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.160
- Oh, the transition below is

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.360
happening. Yeah, it's dripping.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.920
- Yeah, it's dripping on the right.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.600
- And the colder it gets,
the faster it pours.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.120
- Oh wow. That's gorgeous. So fast.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.185
- That's spectacular.

00:01:08.185 --> 00:01:11.360
Superfluid helium can do things that seem impossible.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.640
If you put it in a cup,
a thin film climbs up

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.320
and over the walls, so it empties itself.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.280
You can also use superfluid
helium to create a fountain

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.120
with no moving parts.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.160
It is powered solely by light.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.960
Scientists spent 30 years
looking at this liquid without

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.560
realizing what they had created.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.880
- This is really one
of the great phenomenon

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.320
in 20th century physics,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.880
- And it makes you look at
matter in a different way.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.320
It allows us to explore
the boundary of quantum

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.080
and regular matter.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.960
So we teamed up with the
gravity laboratory at Nottingham

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.280
University to recreate the experiments

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.320
and to find out what really
happens when you just

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.920
keep cooling down.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.280
The obsession with cooling
things down as much

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.480
as possible began in the 1800s.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.280
People noticed that when you
let a gas expand against a

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.080
piston, its temperature drops.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.240
That's because as the
particles hit the piston,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.680
they give it a little push

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.480
and transfer some of their
energy to the piston.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.400
So the particles slow down and
their temperature decreases.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.400
Lord Kelvin learned of this

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.320
and realized it offered a
tantalizing possibility, that

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.400
of an absolute temperature scale.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.440
Imagine that you let the
gas expand an extreme amount

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.040
to the point where all the
particles effectively stop.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.400
The particles no longer
exert any force on the piston

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.920
and they have no more energy left to give.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.520
This would be the absolute
coldest you could get,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.440
absolute zero.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.720
Kelvin quickly computed what
temperature it corresponded to

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.040
and found it was around
negative 273 degrees Celsius.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.080
People speculated

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.440
what strange things might
happen down there, a place

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.440
with no thermal motion.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.360
Kelvin himself suggested that metals,

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.000
which are normally great
conductors of electricity,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.080
might turn into perfect insulators

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.440
and not conduct any electricity at all.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.920
But there was only one way to find out

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.720
and that was you had to get there.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:21.920
Fortunately, the race

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.040
to absolute zero was
already well underway.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.480
It had started by
accident 25 years earlier.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.120
In 1823, Michael Faraday
was playing around

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.720
with chlorine hydrate

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.640
and his scientific mentor suggested

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.120
that heating it in a
sealed tube might lead

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.600
to some interesting results.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.640
So Faraday sealed it
up, warmed it in water,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.320
and watched as a yellow
atmosphere emerged.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.360
Then as the pressure increased
to around four atmospheres,

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.560
he noticed that a bright
yellow fluid formed.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.520
He had just turned chlorine
gas into chlorine liquid.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.080
Faraday then went on a
bit of a liquefying spree,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.360
liquefying six more
gases within five weeks.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.360
But just as he was hitting
his stride, he hit a wall.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.200
Some gases like ethylene
refused to liquefy no matter

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.920
how much he squeezed them.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.360
You see why you need to zoom into a gas.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.680
There you'd find a bunch of
particles whizzing around

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.240
as fast as tiny bullets.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.920
And while it might not seem like it,

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.120
the particles also attract each other.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.560
It's just that they're so spread out

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.960
that they meet relatively little,

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:34.560
and when they do, they're going so fast

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.040
that they mostly just
bounce off each other.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.160
In some cases though, if you
squeeze the particles together

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.040
by increasing the pressure,
you can get those particles

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.360
to stick together and
slide past each other.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.600
In other words, you get a liquid.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.080
This is what happened
with Faraday's chlorine,

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.640
but that doesn't work for all gases.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.960
If the attraction between
particles isn't strong enough

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.680
and the particles are
too hot, then no amount

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.720
of squeezing can get them to liquefy.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.520
And this is what happened with ethylene.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.640
Luckily, there is another way.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.600
If you cool the gas down,
the particles will slow

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.840
and eventually they
start sticking together

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.560
and sliding past each other
and you get your liquid.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.280
So to liquefy more tricky gases,

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.040
Faraday needed colder temperatures
and he got a lucky break.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.920
A French inventor had
discovered how to make dry ice,

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.960
which was about negative
79 degrees Celsius.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.440
So 20 years after his initial chlorine

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.040
success, he tried again.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.640
Faraday put ethylene gas in
a glass tube with a bend,

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.000
compressed it using pumps,

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.920
and then lowered the bend into
a bath of dry ice and ether.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.320
As the dry ice cooled, the ethylene,

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:47.040
a clear colorless liquid formed.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.480
Feraday used the same technique

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.680
and liquefied five more
gases pursuing ever colder

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.200
temperatures in the process

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.640
and getting all the way down
to negative 110 degrees Celsius

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.720
or 163 Kelvin.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:02.720
But six gases still resisted
his attempts, including oxygen,

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.240
hydrogen and nitrogen.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.800
He just couldn't get cold enough.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.320
Over the following decades,

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.080
scientists all over the world picked up

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.920
where Faraday had left off.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:16.160
Liquefying a new gas became
a matter of national pride

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.080
and the race to absolute zero was on.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.160
In 1883, two Polish scientists
liquefied oxygen and nitrogen

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.600
and the record low
temperature fell to 77 Kelvin.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.600
15 years later, James Dewar
liquified hydrogen at 20 Kelvin

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.240
before freezing it at 14.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.000
By the turn of the 20th century,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.840
there was just one gas
left standing, helium,

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:43.680
but some thought helium might
be impossible to liquefy.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.520
And there was good reason to think that

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:48.960
because to turn a gas into
a liquid, its particles have

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.640
to attract each other.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.360
But helium is a very simple element.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.000
Its most common isotope
consists of two protons,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.320
two neutrons and two electrons.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.640
Electrons form shells around the nucleus

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.040
and helium, like all noble
gases, has a full outer shell.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.560
So it isn't really seeking to
gain or lose any electrons,

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.640
and as a result, it doesn't really

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:12.640
bind together with anything.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:15.360
That made people wonder
whether helium atoms

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:16.960
attract each other at all.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.840
And if they didn't, you could
cool down helium as much

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:22.720
as you wanted, but you
would never get a liquid.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.600
And even if the atoms did
attract each other, well,

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.040
the attraction might be so small

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:30.720
that its liquefaction temperature
could be much colder than

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.520
anyone could practically reach.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:38.240
So when Haiku Kamerlingh Onnes
set his sights on liquefying

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.440
helium, he knew he was in for a challenge.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.880
Onnes had joined the race
to absolute zero in 1882,

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.600
and since then he'd been
building an industrial scale

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:51.040
laboratory in Leiden, complete
with chemists, technicians,

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:53.920
glassblowers, and massive liquefiers.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:56.960
By 1906, he too had liquefied hydrogen,

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.440
and from then on, he
dedicated himself fully

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.560
to liquefying helium.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:06.640
But working with helium came
with two major problems.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:09.520
For one thing, just
getting it was difficult.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.080
Remember, helium doesn't
react with anything,

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:15.440
so there are no compounds
you can get helium from,

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:19.200
and the air only contains
around five parts per million.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.240
So on earth, there's almost no helium,

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:24.240
which is at least a little surprising

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.960
because in the universe,
helium is everywhere.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:30.000
It's the second most abundant
element after hydrogen.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.200
The issue is just how light helium is.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:36.320
Release any helium and
it mixes into the air.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:39.920
Over hundreds of thousands
of years, it slowly drifts up

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.800
to the top of the atmosphere
where it gets heated up

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:44.800
by the sun and it speeds up.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.680
Now, a small fraction ends
up going faster than 11

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:51.040
kilometers per second, which
is Earth's escape velocity,

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.120
the velocity required to escape
Earth's gravitational well.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.600
And so it escapes into space.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.800
A much larger fraction gets
ionized by solar radiation

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:02.080
and then gets carried away

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:04.800
by the polar winds along
the Earth's magnetic field

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.360
before ultimately merging
with the solar wind.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.000
And either way, the outcome is the same.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.040
The helium is lost to space forever.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:18.480
So if I pop this balloon, that
helium is effectively gone.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.240
As a result, pure helium at
the time was hard to come by

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:25.280
and the world's total
supply was likely less than

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:27.600
50 party balloons worth.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:31.040
So Onnes had to get his helium
from a different source

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.760
and he turned to sand,
specifically two large bags

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.760
of monazite sand.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.200
That's because monazite is a
mineral that contains around 5%

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.200
thorium by weight and
thorium is radioactive.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.840
When it decays, it spits
out an alpha particle,

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.240
which is two protons and two neutrons.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:53.200
In other words, the nucleus of helium.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:56.160
That nucleus then gets slowed
down by the surrounding rock

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.320
where it steals two electrons
and becomes a helium atom.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:02.880
So over millions of years,
you get this buildup

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.400
of helium atoms trapped inside the rock.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.200
In fact, basically all the
helium we have on earth today

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:11.360
comes from radioactive decay.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.760
It's essentially a radioactive fossil,

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:16.640
although now we mostly get
it from natural gas wells

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.040
where it accumulates over the eons.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:21.280
But Onnes didn't have that luxury.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:24.560
He had to liberate it from the sand.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:26.800
So he recruited four chemists.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.560
They heated up the sand to
release the gas, exploded it

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.880
with oxygen, cooled it with liquid air

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.000
to freeze out impurities,
burned over copper oxide

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.760
to get rid of the hydrogen,

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.320
and last of all,
compressed it over charcoal

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.880
to absorb any remaining impurities.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:46.880
After several months, Onnes
had about 25 party balloons

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:52.560
of pure helium, an estimated
80% of the world supply.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.280
Onnes quickly conducted
careful experiments to try

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:56.880
and tease out whether helium

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.027
atoms attract each other at all.

00:10:59.027 --> 00:11:02.080
- Kamerlingh Onnes did a lot of work

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:04.000
and I think he was becoming more

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:08.320
and more convinced that helium
can in fact be liquefied.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:11.600
- It seemed like the helium
atoms did attract each other

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.440
just ever so slightly.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:16.400
So how do two neutral
atoms that don't react

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:18.480
with anything attract each other?

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:21.760
Well, a helium atom is just
a nucleus with two electrons

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:24.480
around it, but in reality
you never know exactly

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.000
where those electrons are.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.440
So a more accurate picture
looks like this, a nucleus

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:31.920
with an electron cloud around it.

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:35.200
But that cloud isn't always
perfectly evenly distributed.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.560
At any particular instant,

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:38.400
there's slightly more negative charge

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:40.160
on one side than the other.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.280
And now if there's another
helium atom close enough, well,

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.640
this charge imballence can
induce a charge imballence

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:48.240
on that atom.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:53.040
And now the two exert a force
of attraction on each other.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:56.240
This is known as a van der Waals force,

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:59.680
and it's why every atom
attracts every other if

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:01.280
you get it close enough.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.160
But helium holds its two
electrons very tightly,

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:05.760
so the cloud hardly budges

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.640
and the van der Waals force
is tiny, the smallest

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:13.600
of any element, but
perhaps it's just enough.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.560
The second problem with helium
is that no one knew exactly

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:20.000
how cold it would have to get
to liquefy, only that it had

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.360
to be colder than liquid
hydrogen, possibly much colder.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:24.800
And that's the issue

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:28.160
because the rate at which
heat transfers in from the

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:30.640
environment depends on the
temperature difference.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:33.760
The bigger the gap, the more heat flows in

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:36.640
and cooling something down
to possibly a few degrees

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:40.720
above absolute zero while the
room it's in is 290 degrees

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:44.240
above it, well, that's a humongous gap.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:46.800
Fortunately, Dewar had
already invented one

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:49.360
key piece of apparatus.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:53.040
Essentially, one glass
flask nested inside another

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.280
with a vacuum in between.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.960
This limited heat conduction

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.520
to a small connection point at the top.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.640
Convection got ruled out
entirely due to the vacuum

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:04.800
and optionally by covering the insides

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:08.320
with the silver coating, you
could also reflect radiation.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:11.360
This reduced heat losses
by a factor of 30,

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:15.920
and it's the basic design behind
every thermos flask today.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:20.560
But for Onnes, it still wasn't good enough.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:24.720
So he nested several Dewar
flasks inside each other.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:27.520
The helium would go in
the inner one, a bath

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:29.600
of liquid hydrogen would sit around that,

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:32.240
and a bath of liquid air around that.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:35.280
So each layer acted as a
heat shield for the next,

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:39.440
and the helium would see a wall
of 20 Kelvin instead of 300,

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:44.800
cutting the radiated heat
coming in by a factor of 50,000.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.280
To measure the temperature
inside the inner vessel,

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:50.000
Onnes inserted a small glass bulb filled

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:54.080
with dilute helium gas
held at a constant volume.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:56.080
A thin capillary connected this to

00:13:56.080 --> 00:14:00.160
what was essentially a pressure
meter outside the liquefier,

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:02.560
because for a gas at a constant volume,

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:06.080
the temperature is directly
proportional to the pressure.

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.400
So if you first measure
the baseline pressure at a

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:10.640
temperature you know, like
the freezing point of water

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:15.600
around 273 Kelvin, you
could set that to 100%.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:19.280
Then if you later measure a
pressure that's only 10% of

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:21.920
that amount, then the
temperature must also be 10%

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:26.480
of the original or around 27 Kelvin.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:27.760
By using this trick,

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:30.320
HOnnes could measure the
temperature even at a few degrees

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:32.480
above absolute zero.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:35.520
Now in their case, they
read off a level of mercury,

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:39.680
but for simplicity, we'll animate
it as a temperature scale.

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:42.640
Onnes finally had everything ready.

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:45.280
At Professor Weinfurtner's
gravity laboratory, we got

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:46.920
to see the setup we'd use.

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:49.680
- Is this also what it would
have looked like back in the

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:51.240
day when Onnes was doing it?

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:53.600
- Yeah, so this is actually
a really similar setup

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:54.960
to Kamerlingh Onnes.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.280
- The biggest difference
is that we only have two

00:14:57.280 --> 00:14:59.120
Dewars nested inside each other.

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:01.440
The outer one will be
filled with liquid nitrogen

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:04.800
and the inner one is
where the helium will go.

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:08.800
By the 10th of July 1908,
Onnes decided to go for it.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:11.280
He arrived at his lab around 5:30 AM

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:15.360
where his technicians
were already hard at work.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:18.480
They prepared 75 liters of
liquid air the day before

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:22.240
and Onnes now ordered them to
start making liquid hydrogen.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:25.760
By 1:30 PM they had 20
liters, which was enough

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:31.680
to launch their attack on helium.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.800
They started by filling the outer vessels.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:38.000
- First step, we're
going to fill the outer

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.480
Dewar with liquid nitrogen.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:42.560
That's going to precool
the whole system down

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:44.640
to about 77 Kelvin.

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:47.360
Once we're full, we'll
start filling with helium.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:52.000
So we've used about 25

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.640
liters of nitrogen to get to that point.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:59.040
Now consider how big the
Dewar is, that probably holds,

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:02.160
you know, less than 10 liters.

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:04.960
- With the liquid air and
hydrogen in place, they started

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:07.520
to cool the liquefier.

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:11.600
After 30 minutes, it
had cooled to 93 Kelvin.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:14.400
Now it was time to start
cycling the helium.

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:16.880
A pump compressed it to 100 atmospheres

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:19.920
before it went to the liquefier,
where it got precooled to

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:21.840
around 15 Kelvin.

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:25.360
The gas was then rapidly
expanded into the inner vessel,

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:29.120
greatly increasing the
distance between atoms.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:32.640
But remember, helium atoms do
slightly attract each other,

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:35.200
so moving them apart costs energy

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:38.240
and they pay for this with
some of their kinetic energy,

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:40.160
causing the particles to slow down

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:44.160
and their temperature to drop.

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:46.960
The gas then got pumped
back to the compressor,

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:49.760
but the smart thing was that
they let this colder gas cool

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:51.440
the incoming helium,

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:54.720
slightly lowering its
temperature before it expanded.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:56.080
And by doing this over

00:16:56.080 --> 00:17:01.840
and over, the incoming
helium got colder and colder.

00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:06.000
- It's so faint.
- Yeah.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.320
- It feels like a ghostly
fluid or something.

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:10.480
- You know, it's not
liquid that's escaping.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:13.680
It's more of kind of a super dense vapor.

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:18.320
It's only 10 degrees inside,
but it's dropping fast.

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:20.640
- A little after 6:30 PM, Onnes

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:22.640
and his team had gotten
down to six degrees

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:24.400
above absolute zero.

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:26.960
That is closer than anyone had ever gotten

00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:29.680
before, and they kept going.

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:34.800
But just below five Kelvin, the
temperature stopped falling,

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:39.520
so they started to worry that
perhaps something was wrong.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:42.640
But then a chemist in the room
remarked, "It's so stable,

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:45.680
it's almost as if the thermometer
is submerged in a bath

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:50.800
of liquid." So they
rushed to the apparatus,

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:54.720
but still saw nothing
until Onnes grabbed a torch

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:59.680
and shot it from a low angle,
and they saw a reflection.

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:01.520
After the surface had once been seen,

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:04.800
it was no more lost sight of, Onnes wrote,

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:06.960
"It stood out sharply
defined like the edge

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:10.800
of a knife against a glass wall.

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.200
They had just made liquid

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:15.320
helium."

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:18.160
- That's really cool.

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:21.280
It's just hitting the light
on it while it's yeah.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:24.960
So beautiful. It's a,
it's a helium kettle.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:28.560
- Four degrees above absolute zero.

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:31.520
- You can see that really
faint liquid level on the

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:33.118
inside. That's the liquid helium.

00:18:33.118 --> 00:18:36.080
- Oh wow. Okay. So the,
the refractive index

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:38.480
of liquid helium is very
close to air, is it?

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.160
- 1.025. Okay.

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:43.640
- So yeah, really, really
difficult to see by eye. Wow.

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:47.600
- Isn't it crazy to think
that that is about four

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:51.360
degrees above absolute
zero? It doesn't look like.

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:53.680
- It doesn't, there's nothing
that would tip you off.

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:58.800
- Yeah. Back in Leiden,
everyone was elated.

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:01.760
For the next hour, they
ran basic experiments until

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:05.360
by 8:30 PM only 10 millilitres were left.

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:08.800
But then Onnes thought, "Well,
we've made liquid helium.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:12.960
Why not make solid helium?" So
he was going to cool it down,

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:15.120
and the way you can cool down any liquid

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:16.920
is by pumping on it.

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:19.200
- When we pump on it, we're removing all

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:20.560
of the hottest atoms from the system,

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:22.720
which takes about a
third of the fluid away.

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:25.600
So we initially take the level
way higher than we need it

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:27.200
so it can drop down.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:31.280
- So Onnes started his pumps and so did we.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:36.680
- Are we ready?
- Three, two, one, go.

00:19:36.680 --> 00:19:41.520
- Oh,
- Wow. Okay.

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:46.880
- Oh wow. It's gone down so much.

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:49.160
It's gone down like,
what, five centimeters?

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:51.000
- Yeah, immediately.
- Immediately.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:52.800
- Because we're removing all the

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:55.120
hottest atoms from the system.

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:57.000
So we're just shy of 3.5K.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:01.280
- This rapidly evaporating
helium is quickly cooling down.

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:06.320
- So we're about 2.6K. We're below 2.3K.

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:07.560
- Okay.
- Oh,

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:09.820
- It's going super fast, super
- Fast.

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:13.920
- It keeps boiling until
right at 2.17 Kelvin,

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:16.000
it abruptly stops

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:21.040
and the liquid goes
eerily quiet. There it is.

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:24.800
- Ooh. It's completely still again.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:28.160
Man, you can hardly see it. It
feels like a mirror surface.

00:20:28.160 --> 00:20:30.880
It's crazy how smooth that is.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:35.280
- Onnes pumped his helium
down to 1.7 Kelvin,

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:39.680
colder than outer space,
but it didn't freeze.

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:43.280
Instead, it just sat
there, remarkably still.

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:46.160
Onnes concluded it must
just freeze at some lower

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:49.040
temperature, and since
everyone was exhausted,

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:50.960
they called it a day.

00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:57.520
Little did he know what sat
there evaporating in his flask.

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:05.040
Onnes still wanted to freeze helium,

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:08.080
so over the next two years,
he tried several more times,

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:11.040
getting all the way down to one Kelvin,

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:14.240
but all his attempts failed.

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:16.960
This led him to wonder
whether helium will not remain

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:21.600
perhaps liquid even if it is
cooled to the absolute zero.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:24.880
And indeed, scientists later
discovered this is true.

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:26.240
If you take any substance

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:29.920
and keep cooling it down,
eventually it always freezes,

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:32.000
except for helium.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:35.200
Somehow it's the only
substance that never freezes

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:37.680
by just cooling it down.

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:40.560
So Onnes was wise when he gave up on

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:42.080
trying to freeze helium.

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:44.720
Instead, he set out to finally
test Kelvin's prediction

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:46.320
that the electrical conductivity

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:49.200
of metals should stop near absolute zero.

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:51.680
In other words, that their
electrical resistance should

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:53.920
become effectively infinite.

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:57.120
To do this, he submerged
different metals in liquid helium

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:58.800
and measured their resistance.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:01.040
He started with gold and platinum,

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:04.080
but their resistance just
kept slowly decreasing

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:07.920
until finally settling on a steady value.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:11.760
Then on the 8th of April,
1911, he measured mercury

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:17.360
and wrote down just three words

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:20.720
mercury practically zero.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:24.480
As you cooled down, its
resistance slowly decreased until

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:28.560
around 4.2 Kelvin, where
it suddenly plummets to

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:30.800
what appeared to be zero.

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:34.320
Electricity that flowed
with no apparent losses,

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:37.760
the exact opposite of what
Kelvin had hypothesized,

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:41.240
Onnes had just discovered
superconductivity.

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:44.800
- So that's the story most people know.

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:47.760
- Okay. - What most people
don't know is that later

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:51.120
that same day, he also
cooled down the helium more

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:52.880
and he wrote, "Right

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:56.480
before the helium reached
the coldest temperature,

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:01.920
which was 1.8 Kelvin, the
boiling suddenly stopped."

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:03.960
- He noticed.

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:07.360
- He noticed.
- So what did Onnes do

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:09.760
after seeing this sudden change?

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:12.720
Almost nothing. He guessed at an ordinary

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:14.560
explanation and moved on.

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:18.160
But helium kept dropping clues.

00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:19.760
When they measured its density,

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:23.920
they discovered it had a
curious peak around 2.17 Kelvin.

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:26.720
And when they measured the
latent heat, they found that

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:28.720
around the same temperature,
something happened

00:23:28.720 --> 00:23:32.560
to the helium, which was
perhaps even discontinuous.

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:34.080
Yet the biggest smoking gun

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:36.400
of all happened when they
measured a property known

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:39.520
as the specific heat, which
is how much energy it takes

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:42.480
to heat up one gram by one kelvin.

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:44.400
They found that as the
temperature dropped,

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:46.400
the specific heat declined

00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:48.960
until they approached 2.17 Kelvin,

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:51.920
where it suddenly shot up.

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:53.760
This was as clear a sign as any

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:56.360
that something was
happening at this point.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:58.320
- But they knew what this was.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:01.440
This was condensation happening, you know,

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.200
experimental error.

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:04.800
So they had these points,

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:06.240
but they, they wrote like,

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:10.560
"We felt justified in disregarding
these irregular points,

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:14.320
and they only published
the part above 2.61 Kelvin,

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:18.000
which neatly fit the
formula they had for it."

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:20.880
- Onnes brushed off hint

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:25.760
after hint, even while seeing
it happen with his own eyes.

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:28.480
One day, Onnes put one
container of liquid helium

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:32.560
inside another, and he noticed
that below 2.17 Kelvin,

00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:37.280
if one level started higher,
it always quickly evened out.

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:40.560
He even remarked how
strikingly fast it happened.

00:24:40.560 --> 00:24:43.120
He thought it was too
fast for distillation,

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:47.120
but since he had no better
explanation, he stuck with that.

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:49.360
If only he had raised his container,

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:51.600
he might have discovered the truth.

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:55.400
Here, we've got a polymer
cup. This is our container.

00:24:55.400 --> 00:24:59.120
- So we got the bowl loaded up.

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:01.520
It's way at the bottom, and I
think what we're going to try

00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:03.040
and do is lift it up.

00:25:03.040 --> 00:25:05.840
- Yeah. The colder we get, the
better this effect is going

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:09.680
to be, and it's a super subtle
effect at the best of times.

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:13.440
We want to get down to about 1.5, 1.55 K.

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:17.280
I'm going to try and raise it
just above the fluid surface.

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:20.080
- Whoa.
- Can you see that?

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:21.880
- Yeah, yeah,
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:25.040
- Yeah, no, you see like waves almost.

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:29.640
- Yeah. - Yeah. Ah, you can
really see a pulse down there.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:34.000
- If you look at the bottom,
you can see it's dripping.

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:38.080
And if we speed it up, you
can see it keeps dripping.

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:41.920
More and more liquid helium
is going to the bottom.

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:45.360
What's happening is that somehow
the helium is climbing up

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:49.600
and over the walls, literally
emptying itself out.

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:52.480
This was not distillation.

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:57.760
Something far stranger was happening.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.840
Onnes's successors grew suspicious

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:02.960
of the specific heat
data, so they remeasured

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:06.400
and they became convinced
it wasn't an error.

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:10.880
This was real. Something was
happening at 2.17 Kelvin.

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:13.440
Since the curve resembled
the Greek letter Lambda,

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:15.520
they called this the Lambda point

00:26:15.520 --> 00:26:18.720
and they suggested that a phase
transition must be happening

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:22.160
here, but not an ordinary
phase transition.

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:25.440
This would be a phase
transition to a more exotic kind

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:29.760
of matter, from a normal
liquid to something else.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:30.803
But what?

00:26:30.803 --> 00:26:36.800
Figuring out what's really happening when there are so many different
clues can be confusing.

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:40.640
If not, outright impossible.
The same is still true today.

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:44.240
Recently, I came across this
story where scientists used AI

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:46.800
to design viruses not found in nature,

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:48.720
but depending on where you read the story,

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:50.800
you might have a completely
different reaction.

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:53.040
If you read IFLS first,

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:55.840
you might think this is an
exciting scientific breakthrough,

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:59.120
AI designing a virus
that can kill bacteria.

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:02.080
If you'd seen this first,
you'd get a more complete,

00:27:02.080 --> 00:27:05.760
albeit complex picture,
16 viruses from scratch,

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:07.840
but with possible downsides.

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:10.160
And if you saw this New
York Post article first,

00:27:10.160 --> 00:27:13.040
you'd most likely focus
on what could go wrong.

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:14.160
So three headlines,

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:16.880
but three completely
different conclusions.

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:19.040
Most people just get their
news from one source,

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:22.320
and as a result, their takeaway
is a little bit like the

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:25.680
luck of the draw, which outlet
did you happen to see first?

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:28.000
That's why we use today's
sponsor, Ground News.

