00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:33.200
of the video, and now back to

00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:36.720
what was happening when you
keep cooling down helium.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:39.720
The answer started to take
shape throughout the 1930s.

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:41.920
- So in 1945,

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:45.120
there was this graduate
student called Don Misener,

00:28:45.120 --> 00:28:47.600
and he needed to do some experiments

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:50.000
for his coursework to get enough credit.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:53.440
And he was aware of everything
that was happening in helium,

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:56.480
but he realized that no one
had measured its viscosity.

00:28:56.480 --> 00:28:57.440
So he's like, "Oh, that's&nbsp;

00:28:57.440 --> 00:29:02.240
weird. I will measure it."
 
 
- So how do you measure the viscosity?

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:08.320
- There are two ways. You have a disc

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:13.600
and you try to see what the
resistance of the liquid is.

00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:18.240
Another way is to have a small

00:29:18.240 --> 00:29:23.440
capillary, and then you push
the liquid through that,

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:27.600
and the viscosity you measure

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:32.560
between the two methods
should be exactly the same.

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:36.160
- Misener chose a variation
of the first method.

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:38.560
He submerged a cylinder that was connected

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:41.440
through a torsion fiber in liquid helium.

00:29:41.440 --> 00:29:42.880
He then rotated the cylinder

00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:45.440
and let go, causing the
cylinder to swing back

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:47.440
and forth to oscillate.

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:49.280
Now, by watching how long it oscillated,

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:50.960
he could determine the viscosity.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:53.360
The longer it oscillated,
the less friction it must

00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:55.840
experience, so the lower the viscosity.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:58.160
And what he found was that at 4.2 Kelvin,

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:00.160
the viscosity was about 14%.

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:02.640
That of air, already tiny.

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:04.560
Then, as he decreased the temperature,

00:30:04.560 --> 00:30:07.040
the viscosity slowly increased

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:10.800
until at the transition point,
it suddenly plummeted, not

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:14.560
to zero, but to about 5% that of air.

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.640
This caught the attention of Jack Allen, a

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:19.680
27-year-old physicist who'd
just come to Cambridge.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:22.240
Allen had inherited a helium
liquefier from someone

00:30:22.240 --> 00:30:23.680
who could no longer use it,

00:30:23.680 --> 00:30:25.200
and now he resolved to use it

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:27.680
to unravel the mysteries of helium.

00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:29.520
Of course, he could use some help,

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:32.480
and Misener seemed like
the perfect PhD student,

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:33.760
so Allen convinced him,

00:30:33.760 --> 00:30:37.520
and together, they continued
studying helium's viscosity.

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:40.560
Only now they turn to the
second method of studying it,

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:42.640
which works something like this.

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:45.520
I've got a tube filled with
two layers of glycerol.

00:30:45.520 --> 00:30:47.920
Now, if I let it flow,
what's going to happen?

00:30:47.920 --> 00:30:51.040
Well, you'd expect the layers
to drop, which they do,

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:53.040
but not in a straightforward way.

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:54.720
It doesn't maintain its shape.

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:57.440
Instead, it stretches out in the middle.

00:30:57.440 --> 00:30:59.920
What's happening is that
if you zoom in, you'd find

00:30:59.920 --> 00:31:02.000
that the molecules near
the wall get stuck,

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:03.280
they experience friction,

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:05.760
and then the molecules
sliding past them experience

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:07.360
friction with those molecules.

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:11.600
The internal friction is
basically what viscosity is.

00:31:11.600 --> 00:31:13.600
In general, if you make
the channel smaller,

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:15.280
the flow rate should decrease,

00:31:15.280 --> 00:31:18.160
and if you push on the
liquid, it should increase.

00:31:18.160 --> 00:31:19.840
Now, by carefully keeping track of

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:22.640
how things change when you
alter these parameters,

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:24.320
you can determine the viscosity.

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:26.400
In November of 1937, Allen

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:28.320
and Misener did a version
of this experiment

00:31:28.320 --> 00:31:30.800
with liquid helium at around one kelvin.

00:31:30.800 --> 00:31:33.120
They let it flow through
one wide capillary

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:36.160
and one narrow one, and they
found that the flow speed

00:31:36.160 --> 00:31:38.160
through both was almost the same,

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:40.800
despite the narrow one
having a surface area a

00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:42.720
thousand times as small.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:43.840
Even weirder was

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:47.120
that the speed hardly changed
when the pressure increased.

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:50.240
They concluded that any known
formula cannot, from our data,

00:31:50.240 --> 00:31:51.920
give a value of the viscosity,

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:53.680
which would have much meaning.

00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:57.280
As far as they could tell, the
viscosity was non-existent.

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:00.800
- They know they've got gold
on their hands here, right?

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:04.800
They know this is a, like
a bombshell of a result.

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:07.680
- So they spent the next three
weeks making sure they hadn't

00:32:07.680 --> 00:32:09.040
made any mistakes.

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:10.400
They repeated all the experiments

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:12.000
and did all the other measurements they

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:14.080
needed, ready to write it up.

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:16.640
- But then on the last day, one

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:19.280
of Kapitsa's old students walks in

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:20.960
with a letter from Kapitsa.

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:24.800
- "Who's Kapitsa? Well,
remember that allen had inherited

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:27.600
that liquefier from someone
who couldn't use it anymore?

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:31.040
Well, that was Soviet
physicist Pyotr Kapitsa.

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:35.920
He built the liquefier and
ran the lab before allen.

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:38.960
But then during a trip back
home in the summer of 1934,

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:42.000
Stalin had his passport confiscated.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:44.880
He was to work and stay
in the Soviet Union, where

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:48.080
for the past few years, Kapitsa
had been rebuilding his lab,

00:32:48.080 --> 00:32:51.520
telling almost no one exactly
what he was working on.

00:32:51.520 --> 00:32:54.920
But Allen and Misener
were about to find out.

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:59.520
- He had done the same sort of experiment,

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:02.400
found the viscosity was vanishingly small,

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:05.360
and send a note to the editor of Nature

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:08.400
that he would be very grateful
if they could publish it

00:33:08.400 --> 00:33:10.560
as soon as possible.

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:13.200
Don't bother to send the
proofs to me here to Moscow.

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:17.440
It takes too much time. He had
just scooped this old laugh.

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:20.720
- He knew what he had
and he raced to publish.

00:33:20.720 --> 00:33:23.280
Allen and Misener were freaking out,

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:26.000
but over the next four days,
they wrote up all their results

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:29.280
and also sent it to nature.

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:32.640
On the 8th of January, 1938,
the two papers appeared side

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:37.200
by side, reporting the
vanishingly small viscosity,

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:39.200
perhaps even zero.

00:33:39.200 --> 00:33:41.840
And then in analogy to superconductors,

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:45.680
Kapitsa called it a superfluid.

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:48.480
This was the breakthrough
everyone had been waiting for

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:50.560
because it finally began to explain some

00:33:50.560 --> 00:33:53.280
of the strange things they'd been seeing.

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:56.960
Above 2.17 Kelvin, helium
is just a normal fluid

00:33:56.960 --> 00:33:58.320
and it has internal friction

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:01.760
or viscosity, so when it flows
through tiny cracks, part

00:34:01.760 --> 00:34:04.080
of it gets stuck to the walls.

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:06.480
Now, in the case of the
bucket, the bottom is made

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:08.480
of a polymer aerogel, which is filled

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:12.560
with microscopic channels up
to tens of nanometers across.

00:34:12.560 --> 00:34:13.440
That's so small

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:16.160
that the helium gets fully
clamped to the walls.

00:34:16.160 --> 00:34:18.240
It can't flow through.

00:34:18.240 --> 00:34:21.600
But as you cool down below 2.17 Kelvin,

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:24.000
the helium transforms,

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:26.880
and now it can suddenly
pass through tiny cracks

00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:31.760
with no friction, so it leaks
straight through the bottom.

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:36.800
It also explained what was
happening with the little cup.

00:34:36.800 --> 00:34:39.920
How? Well, remember
those van der Waals forces

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:42.560
that cause every atom
to attract every other?

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:45.440
Well, between helium
atoms, they're very weak,

00:34:45.440 --> 00:34:46.640
but the atoms in the walls

00:34:46.640 --> 00:34:49.520
of the cup have many more
electrons in their outer shell,

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:52.480
so the charge imballences become larger.

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:55.120
And as a result, the helium
atoms are much more strongly

00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:57.920
drawn to the wall than to each other.

00:34:57.920 --> 00:34:59.600
So they climb up the wall

00:34:59.600 --> 00:35:02.800
and within seconds wet the
entire surface with a layer

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:05.920
that's around 30 nanometers thick.

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:08.480
And because the bottom
of this thin sheet is

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:12.480
below the liquid layer,
it now acts as a siphon.

00:35:12.480 --> 00:35:14.160
Normal fluid can't flow through

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:16.400
because it experiences friction,

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:20.160
but superfluid can, so it flows up

00:35:20.160 --> 00:35:25.120
and over the walls until
it drips out the bottom.

00:35:31.040 --> 00:35:34.080
But for all the idea of
a superfluid explained,

00:35:34.080 --> 00:35:37.200
it also raised a much
more fundamental question.

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:38.880
How do you get a superfluid?

00:35:38.880 --> 00:35:42.000
- Like if it is actually zero,
I want you to think about

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:43.680
how crazy that is, right?

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:47.360
Because if we think back to,
okay, what is this viscosity?

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:49.680
It's internal friction
between the molecules.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:53.040
So if you have actually
zero viscosity, it means

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:55.200
that you have like a billion,

00:35:55.200 --> 00:35:58.160
billion particles all
traveling through this pipe

00:35:58.160 --> 00:36:01.920
and not a single one is
bouncing off of anything

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.240
and losing some energy or bouncing into

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:06.000
each other and losing energy.

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:09.280
They all must flow perfectly

00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:12.240
and somehow not lose a
single bit of energy.

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:15.280
It seems preposterous.

00:36:15.280 --> 00:36:17.360
- Within two months of the nature papers,

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:20.000
Fritz London proposed
a possible mechanism.

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:23.040
There was this forgotten
paper from 13 years earlier.

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:25.600
It predicted that if you just
kept cooling down a certain

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:27.600
type of particle, then
the atoms would fall

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:29.360
into an exotic state.

00:36:29.360 --> 00:36:32.160
And the author of that
forgotten paper was none other

00:36:32.160 --> 00:36:34.080
than Albert Einstein.

00:36:34.080 --> 00:36:36.160
To understand what Einstein had predicted,

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:38.240
consider this plane of particles.

00:36:38.240 --> 00:36:39.520
They're not interacting

00:36:39.520 --> 00:36:42.000
and they're free to move
around in every direction

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:44.960
with different velocities and
different kinetic energies.

00:36:44.960 --> 00:36:47.280
Now at room temperature,
you can just think of them

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:49.520
as point particles,

00:36:49.520 --> 00:36:51.440
but if you start cooling them down,

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:53.440
what Einstein realized is that, well,

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:55.200
this is no longer the case

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:57.760
because now quantum theory
starts to play a role.

00:36:57.760 --> 00:36:59.840
And according to that
theory, particles aren't just

00:36:59.840 --> 00:37:02.200
particles, they also have
a wave-like character.

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:05.600
- So-called wave particle
duality, the quantum

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:08.160
wave function tells you the likelihood of

00:37:08.160 --> 00:37:09.440
where it would be found if you were

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:11.040
to try to measure its location.

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:13.600
- The important thing here
is that the wavelength

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:16.560
of these waves or how
spread out they are is equal

00:37:16.560 --> 00:37:18.560
to planks constant over their momentum

00:37:18.560 --> 00:37:20.040
or mass times velocity.

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:22.720
- So if you're cooling
these particles down,

00:37:22.720 --> 00:37:23.760
they on average have less

00:37:23.760 --> 00:37:27.120
and less momentum per particle,
their quantum wavelength

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:30.000
or the de Broglie wavelength gets longer.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.760
And so eventually the sort
of quantum state associated

00:37:33.760 --> 00:37:36.800
with each of those seemingly
independent particles starts

00:37:36.800 --> 00:37:38.880
to smoosh out and overlap.

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:42.600
This distinguishability in
space starts to melt away.

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:45.680
- What happens next depends on which kind

00:37:45.680 --> 00:37:47.680
of particle you're cooling down.

00:37:47.680 --> 00:37:50.480
Every particle in the
universe is one of two kinds.

00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:53.200
It's either a boson or a fermion.

00:37:53.200 --> 00:37:55.760
Fermions are most of the
particles you're familiar with,

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:57.920
like protons, neutrons and electrons,

00:37:57.920 --> 00:38:00.560
and bosons are particles like photons.

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:02.480
You can see the difference between bosons

00:38:02.480 --> 00:38:06.640
and fermions by looking at two
indistinguishable particles.

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:10.080
Now, one particle just
has its own wave function,

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:12.080
but if you take two of them together,

00:38:12.080 --> 00:38:13.840
something special happens.

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:15.440
They get a combined wave function

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:18.800
that describes both of them together.

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:21.840
In the case of bosons, if you
could see that wave function,

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:24.240
you'd see it's the same on both sides,

00:38:24.240 --> 00:38:26.960
that is it's symmetric.

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:28.960
But if those particles are fermions,

00:38:28.960 --> 00:38:30.880
then their combined wave function is the

00:38:30.880 --> 00:38:32.480
opposite on both sides.

00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:35.200
It's anti-symmetric.

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:37.440
Now I want you to think about
what this means if you try

00:38:37.440 --> 00:38:39.760
to move those particles together.

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:41.760
In the case of the bosons, the two sides

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:43.360
of the wave function add up

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:46.960
and you just get two bosons
in the same place or state.

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:49.440
But now try doing the same for fermions.

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:52.160
You get one peak to
overlap with one valley

00:38:52.160 --> 00:38:54.080
and they'd cancel out.

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:55.360
But that would suggest that all

00:38:55.360 --> 00:38:57.520
of a sudden there's a 0% probability

00:38:57.520 --> 00:38:59.440
of finding the particles anywhere,

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:00.960
which doesn't make any sense.

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:02.880
They have to be somewhere. So

00:39:02.880 --> 00:39:04.480
what this is really telling us is

00:39:04.480 --> 00:39:07.440
that two indistinguishable
fermions can never occupy

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:09.520
the same quantum state.

00:39:09.520 --> 00:39:12.240
This is known as Pauli's
exclusion principle,

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:14.880
and it's why particles like
electrons can never be in

00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:16.640
exactly the same state.

00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:18.880
If the particles you're
cooling down are fermions,

00:39:18.880 --> 00:39:21.840
then they start filling up the
energy states like a ladder

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:24.400
from lowest to the higher states,

00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:27.120
with only one fermion per quantum state.

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:29.600
But bosons don't have this restriction.

00:39:29.600 --> 00:39:32.880
They're very happy to share
the same quantum state.

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:34.720
When you keep cooling them down, more

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:36.720
and more of them start to occupy lower

00:39:36.720 --> 00:39:39.600
and lower energy levels.

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:41.440
And Einstein realized there comes a point

00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:45.040
where a macroscopic portion
suddenly condenses into the

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:46.400
lowest energy state

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:49.520
or ground state, which
in this case is just when

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:51.080
they're completely still.

00:39:51.080 --> 00:39:55.200
- These little particles
could actually act in a way

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:57.520
where they would sort of
lose, so to speak, their,

00:39:57.520 --> 00:40:00.000
their individuality, that they could act

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.880
as a single coherent state
of matter, a whole new state

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:07.600
of matter that had no analog.

00:40:07.600 --> 00:40:10.480
- This became known as a
bose Einstein condensate

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:13.840
after Bose whose work
Einstein was building on.

00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:16.720
But Einstein doubted this
could happen in real life.

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:18.560
He wrote, "The theory is pretty,

00:40:18.560 --> 00:40:23.440
but is there also some truth
to it?" 13 years later,

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:24.800
London believed there might be

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:27.360
and that perhaps superfluid helium was a

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:29.120
bose Einstein condensate.

00:40:29.120 --> 00:40:31.200
At first, that might seem impossible

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:33.520
because helium is made
up of protons, neutrons,

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:36.560
and electrons, and every
one of those is a fermion.

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:39.520
So you'd expect helium
to be a fermion too.

00:40:39.520 --> 00:40:42.960
And remember, fermions
can't share the same state,

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:46.480
but helium is not a fermion.

00:40:46.480 --> 00:40:49.760
bosons and fermions differ
in one other fundamental way,

00:40:49.760 --> 00:40:52.400
their intrinsic angular momentum or spin.

00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:56.000
For bosons, this is always
an integer number, zero, one,

00:40:56.000 --> 00:40:57.760
two, three, and so on.

00:40:57.760 --> 00:41:01.680
But fermions always have a
half integer spin, like a half.

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:04.240
So what happens if you
have multiple fermions

00:41:04.240 --> 00:41:06.160
together, say in one atom?

00:41:06.160 --> 00:41:08.000
Well, now you have to
add their spins together

00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:10.560
to get an overall spin for the atom,

00:41:10.560 --> 00:41:14.320
and then use that to see whether
it's a bozon or a fermion.

00:41:14.320 --> 00:41:18.000
In the case of helium, which
has six half spin particles,

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:20.080
then no matter how you add them up,

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:22.880
you always end up with an integer number.

00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:27.200
As a result, helium
atoms behave as bosons.

00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:30.160
And as you cool them
down below 2.17 Kelvin,

00:41:30.160 --> 00:41:31.440
a macroscopic fraction

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:34.960
of the atoms condense into
the lowest energy state.

00:41:34.960 --> 00:41:37.280
They lose their individuality

00:41:37.280 --> 00:41:42.720
and about a trillion trillion
atoms all behave as one.

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:44.400
Normally, you think of wave functions

00:41:44.400 --> 00:41:45.920
as these teeny tiny things,

00:41:45.920 --> 00:41:49.520
but here it's literally
the size of the container.

00:41:49.520 --> 00:41:53.680
It's as if after the transition,
the helium has turned into

00:41:53.680 --> 00:42:00.040
one big atom with all these
atoms behaving as one.

00:42:00.040 --> 00:42:04.320
- This is quantum mechanics
with your own eyes.

00:42:04.840 --> 00:42:08.480
- And it makes you look at
matter in a different way.

00:42:08.480 --> 00:42:11.920
It allows us to explore
the boundary of quantum

00:42:11.920 --> 00:42:14.400
and regular matter.

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:16.480
So it seems like we've solved it.

00:42:16.480 --> 00:42:19.040
The atoms are in the lowest energy state.

00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:20.640
They've lost their individuality

00:42:20.640 --> 00:42:22.960
and act as one, so they can't scatter

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:28.560
and they flow with zero internal friction.

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:30.880
That's how I first understood superfluids

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:33.440
and how I'd explain it to
anyone who wanted to know.

00:42:33.440 --> 00:42:36.320
I mean, it made sense and we're all happy.

00:42:36.320 --> 00:42:38.880
The only problem is that it's wrong.

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:41.760
I mean, the first issue is that in a BEC,

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:43.440
all the atoms occupy the state

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:45.440
where the particles are at rest,

00:42:45.440 --> 00:42:48.480
but in a superfluid, well, they flow.

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:50.400
Maybe that issue could be excused

00:42:50.400 --> 00:42:53.040
because you could imagine moving
along with the superfluid

00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:56.400
and finding a frame in which
it effectively stands still,

00:42:56.400 --> 00:42:58.720
but there's an even
bigger problem, which is

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:02.000
that in a non-interacting
Bows Einstein condensate,

00:43:02.000 --> 00:43:05.760
even the tiniest push can
excite any of the atoms.

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:07.440
So if you'd try to let it flow,

00:43:07.440 --> 00:43:09.280
the smallest disturbance would knock

00:43:09.280 --> 00:43:10.960
atoms out of the ground state.

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:14.000
They would scatter and the
flow would lose energy.

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:17.200
So clearly a BEC alone isn't enough.

00:43:17.200 --> 00:43:20.800
You need something else, but what?

00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:22.320
Fortunately, Kapitsa knew

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:24.560
exactly who could solve the problem.

00:43:24.560 --> 00:43:26.400
Unfortunately, he had been locked

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:28.920
inside Stalin's prison for the last year.

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:32.560
- This was right around the
time of Stalin's great terror

00:43:32.560 --> 00:43:34.640
where he was executing

00:43:34.640 --> 00:43:37.360
or imprisoning all the enemies of state,

00:43:37.360 --> 00:43:39.640
whether they were real or imaginary.

00:43:39.640 --> 00:43:41.840
- But in the case of Lev Landau,

00:43:41.840 --> 00:43:44.000
the physicist Kapitsa was thinking about,

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:46.800
it seems like there was some truth to it.

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:49.120
Landau was known as a genius theorist

00:43:49.120 --> 00:43:52.080
who cared deeply about
elevating Soviet physics.

00:43:52.080 --> 00:43:53.600
He was the head of the physics department

00:43:53.600 --> 00:43:54.880
and personally trained some

00:43:54.880 --> 00:43:57.440
of the best physicists of the century.

00:43:57.440 --> 00:43:59.200
But around the mid 1930s,

00:43:59.200 --> 00:44:02.000
he became disillusioned
with Stalin's regime.

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:04.800
He reportedly told one colleague
that Soviet officials were

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:09.680
"born subhuman." And according
to KGB Files, he was accused

00:44:09.680 --> 00:44:13.360
of heading a counter-revolutionary
organization.

00:44:13.360 --> 00:44:16.480
Landau was arrested on
the 28th of April 1938

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:20.480
and taken to the Lubyanka Prison.

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:23.440
The first day he was forced
to stand for seven hours,

00:44:23.440 --> 00:44:27.680
followed by six days of
12-hour persuasion sessions.

00:44:27.680 --> 00:44:29.360
After two months, Landau broke

00:44:29.360 --> 00:44:31.360
and wrote a six-page confession.

00:44:31.360 --> 00:44:33.360
It reads, "We came to the conclusion

00:44:33.360 --> 00:44:34.800
that the party had degenerated

00:44:34.800 --> 00:44:37.360
and that the Soviet government
no longer acted in the

00:44:37.360 --> 00:44:39.440
interests of workers, but in the interests

00:44:39.440 --> 00:44:41.440
of a small ruling group.

00:44:41.440 --> 00:44:43.760
The interests of the country
demanded the overthrow

00:44:43.760 --> 00:44:45.200
of the existing government."

00:44:45.200 --> 00:44:48.880
- That's a pretty,
pretty strong confession.

00:44:48.880 --> 00:44:52.400
- This confession at a time
where people were being executed

00:44:52.400 --> 00:44:56.080
for no reason should have
meant the end for Landau.

00:44:56.080 --> 00:44:59.200
But fortunately for him, he
had someone defending him.

00:44:59.200 --> 00:45:00.800
The morning Landau got arrested,

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:03.360
Kapitsa immediately wrote to Stalin.

00:45:03.360 --> 00:45:05.840
His character, to put it simply, is nasty.

00:45:05.840 --> 00:45:07.760
He is a bully and a brawler,

00:45:07.760 --> 00:45:10.160
but I find it very hard to
believe that Landau was capable

00:45:10.160 --> 00:45:11.920
of anything disOnnes.

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:14.400
Landau is young and he still has much

00:45:14.400 --> 00:45:16.000
to accomplish in science.

00:45:16.000 --> 00:45:17.840
No one else can write about all of this,

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:20.800
which is why I'm writing to you.

00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:22.960
Now, Stalin never replied.

00:45:22.960 --> 00:45:24.880
So a year later, Kapitsa wrote to one

00:45:24.880 --> 00:45:26.720
of Stalin's closest allies.

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:29.120
He told him how he had
discovered superfluidity

00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:33.280
and that no theorist other
than Landau could explain it.

00:45:33.280 --> 00:45:36.080
Three weeks later, Kapitsa
was summoned to the prison.

00:45:36.080 --> 00:45:38.720
As he recalled, "I was
summoned at 1:00 AM.

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:42.320
They escorted me to a big
office occupied by two men.

00:45:42.320 --> 00:45:44.480
Do you understand for
whom you are pleading?

00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:47.040
He's a most dangerous
criminal, a spy who confessed

00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:50.560
to everything." We talked
till four in the morning.

00:45:50.560 --> 00:45:53.280
Towards the end, one of them
said, "All right, Kapitsa,

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:54.960
if you pledge your word for Landau,

00:45:54.960 --> 00:45:56.960
then give us a written guarantee.

00:45:56.960 --> 00:46:00.560
If anything happens, you
will be held responsible."

00:46:00.560 --> 00:46:02.320
Kapitsa wrote the letter

00:46:02.320 --> 00:46:05.680
and two days later, Landau
turned up at the institute, ready

00:46:05.680 --> 00:46:11.200
to get back to what he
loved doing most, physics.

00:46:11.200 --> 00:46:13.440
Kapitsa had put his neck on the line.

00:46:13.440 --> 00:46:16.720
Now it was up to Landau to
prove he was up to the task,

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:20.640
and superfluid helium had
only gotten more interesting.

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:23.200
Back in January 1938, just days

00:46:23.200 --> 00:46:25.440
after the nature papers, allen was studying

00:46:25.440 --> 00:46:27.760
how superfluid helium conducts heat.

00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:30.160
To do this, he packed some Emery powder,

00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:33.920
basically fine sand, in a
glass bulb with an open bottom.

00:46:33.920 --> 00:46:37.520
To see what was happening, he
shone his pocket torch at it.

00:46:37.520 --> 00:46:40.880
And according to him, as the
Emery powder tube came into

00:46:40.880 --> 00:46:44.400
view, helium spurted
out the top of the tube.

00:46:44.400 --> 00:46:45.920
It continued to spurt as long

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:47.680
as the light shone on the powder

00:46:47.680 --> 00:46:50.880
and ceased when a card cut off the light.

00:46:50.880 --> 00:46:53.600
allen later showed this
fountain with no moving parts

00:46:53.600 --> 00:46:56.880
to the Royal Society, during
which famous writer H.G.

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:58.800
Wells crawled under the table looking

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:01.440
for a hidden pump, but found nothing.

00:47:01.440 --> 00:47:04.560
- Yeah, so we had this
kind of specialist made.

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:05.760
It's a single piece of glass.

00:47:05.760 --> 00:47:07.360
There are no seams in it whatsoever.

00:47:07.360 --> 00:47:09.520
The filtered disc is fused in,

00:47:09.520 --> 00:47:11.920
and then it's just filled
with carbon stones,

00:47:11.920 --> 00:47:13.520
and we just hit it with a bright light

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:15.760
that heats it up, drives
the fountain flow.

00:47:15.760 --> 00:47:20.240
- Amazing. Fingers
crossed. You guys ready?

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:24.320
Three, two, one. Torch it.

00:47:24.320 --> 00:47:31.920
Woah! Yeah.

00:47:31.920 --> 00:47:36.160
Let's go. What?

00:47:36.160 --> 00:47:37.200
That's absurd.

00:47:37.200 --> 00:47:43.800
- That's awesome. Woo. Let's go.

00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:47.680
- So what was happening?

00:47:47.680 --> 00:47:51.240
How could light cause
the helium to shoot out?

00:47:51.240 --> 00:47:55.120
- Any theory now had to take

00:47:55.120 --> 00:47:59.040
into consideration all these phenomena.

00:47:59.040 --> 00:48:03.800
So for theoreticians
was a really heavy task.

00:48:03.800 --> 00:48:07.600
- Landau spent the next
eight years working on it.

00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:10.240
He started by considering a
superfluid flowing at absolute

00:48:10.240 --> 00:48:13.760
zero, and he realized
what London had left out,

00:48:13.760 --> 00:48:16.000
the forces between atoms.

00:48:16.000 --> 00:48:18.720
In a Bose Einstein condensate,
there are no forces

00:48:18.720 --> 00:48:20.800
between particles, and as a result,

00:48:20.800 --> 00:48:22.720
a push can knock a single atom out

00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:26.080
of the lowest energy state,
resulting in easy friction.

00:48:26.080 --> 00:48:28.720
But in liquid helium,
this is not the case.

00:48:28.720 --> 00:48:31.360
The atoms interact, so if you push on one,

00:48:31.360 --> 00:48:33.280
you're pushing on a bunch of them.

00:48:33.280 --> 00:48:35.600
Landau realized that this rigidity meant

00:48:35.600 --> 00:48:38.720
that the only way you can
create excitations in a superfluid

00:48:38.720 --> 00:48:40.640
and lose energy in the flow is

00:48:40.640 --> 00:48:43.440
through the collective
motion of the atoms.

00:48:43.440 --> 00:48:47.360
He argued that these excitations
come in two main flavors.

00:48:47.360 --> 00:48:50.080
The first is that if you push
hard enough, you get the atoms

00:48:50.080 --> 00:48:51.120
to bump into each other

00:48:51.120 --> 00:48:53.360
and create ripples throughout the fluid,

00:48:53.360 --> 00:48:56.160
basically sound waves,
just a quantized version

00:48:56.160 --> 00:48:58.000
of them called phonons.

00:48:58.000 --> 00:49:00.640
Similar to how photons
are particles of light,

00:49:00.640 --> 00:49:03.520
these are particles of sound.

00:49:03.520 --> 00:49:06.880
The second kind of excitation
is something known as a roton,

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:10.400
because originally people
thought of them as tiny whirls,

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:13.040
but now they're seeing
more like a patch of atoms

00:49:13.040 --> 00:49:15.760
that suddenly orders themselves
into a crystal lattice just

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:17.200
for a split second.

00:49:17.200 --> 00:49:20.640
You can think of them almost
as tiny ghosts of a solid.

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:23.840
And just like a phonon
creating a roton costs both

00:49:23.840 --> 00:49:25.440
energy and momentum.

00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:27.600
So Landau argued that these phonons

00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:29.280
and rotons were the only way

00:49:29.280 --> 00:49:31.520
that the flow could lose energy.

00:49:31.520 --> 00:49:34.960
- And you're like, okay,
well, it's quite easy,

00:49:34.960 --> 00:49:36.160
you know, to make a sound wave.

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:38.560
Like all I have to do
is knock on it, right?

00:49:38.560 --> 00:49:41.200
That in terms of like
how much energy it takes

00:49:41.200 --> 00:49:44.160
to create a phonon, it
can be very little energy.

00:49:44.160 --> 00:49:46.400
It can be as little energy as you want,

00:49:46.400 --> 00:49:48.960
but you don't just have
to conserve energy.

00:49:48.960 --> 00:49:50.800
You also have to conserve momentum.

00:49:50.800 --> 00:49:52.240
So when you push on it

00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:55.360
or when the liquid bumps
into something, it both needs

00:49:55.360 --> 00:49:57.120
to satisfy conservation of energy

00:49:57.120 --> 00:49:59.360
and conservation of momentum.

00:49:59.360 --> 00:50:04.080
And there is a very nice
relationship that tells you exactly

00:50:04.080 --> 00:50:06.880
how the momentum of a phonon
corresponds to the energy

00:50:06.880 --> 00:50:10.240
of a phonon, and it's
connected with the speed

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:12.480
of sound in a material.

00:50:12.480 --> 00:50:15.760
And so the only way you can
satisfy both constraints is if

00:50:15.760 --> 00:50:19.200
the flow is going at
least the speed of sound,

00:50:19.200 --> 00:50:21.600
which in helium two is very fast.

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:25.280
It's about, I think, 220
or 240 meters per second.

00:50:25.280 --> 00:50:29.120
Rotons, he realizes, can get
created a little bit easier.

00:50:29.120 --> 00:50:31.200
I think the modern value is about 60

00:50:31.200 --> 00:50:33.400
meters per second, but it's still a lot.

00:50:33.400 --> 00:50:36.960
- So in Landau's theory, below
about 60 meters per second,

00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:40.240
the flow can't create a
single phonon or roton.

00:50:40.240 --> 00:50:42.000
The atoms all stay in the ground state

00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:43.840
and no energy is lost.

00:50:43.840 --> 00:50:45.840
So it's not that the
friction is vanishingly

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:47.280
small in a superfluid.

00:50:47.280 --> 00:50:50.160
Landau proved that it's strictly zero.

00:50:50.160 --> 00:50:52.000
Friction is forbidden.

00:50:52.000 --> 00:50:55.200
In practice, other excitations
are created at lower speeds

00:50:55.200 --> 00:50:58.080
and therefore the actual flow
rate at which superfluidity

00:50:58.080 --> 00:51:00.480
breaks down is lower.

00:51:00.480 --> 00:51:02.720
But if the friction is actually zero,

00:51:02.720 --> 00:51:04.480
that implies something else.

00:51:04.480 --> 00:51:07.440
You should be able to get a
superfluid flowing in a loop,

00:51:07.440 --> 00:51:10.560
and once it's going, it
should just keep going like a

00:51:10.560 --> 00:51:12.240
perpetual motion machine.

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:15.680
In 1964, two physicists
at Yale tested this.

00:51:15.680 --> 00:51:18.400
They got superfluid
helium circling in a stack

00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:20.480
of thin rings and waited.

00:51:20.480 --> 00:51:21.760
And what they found was

00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:24.160
that no matter whether
they waited five minutes

00:51:24.160 --> 00:51:28.800
or more than 12 hours, the
flow was just as strong.

00:51:28.800 --> 00:51:32.160
- But then, you know,
if the friction is zero,

00:51:32.160 --> 00:51:34.160
how is it possible that Misener, you know,

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:35.600
when he did his like torsion

00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:37.560
experiment, he didn't measure zero.

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:40.560
- The liquid wasn't just
passing over liquid,

00:51:40.560 --> 00:51:42.760
like you had like a cylinder
of something in there?

00:51:42.760 --> 00:51:45.680
- Yep. - Yep. I don't know.
Is it coming from the wire?

00:51:45.680 --> 00:51:47.040
Is it coming from the cylinder?

00:51:47.040 --> 00:51:49.760
Like the different materials?
What is it coming from?

00:51:49.760 --> 00:51:51.600
- Your instinct is

00:51:51.600 --> 00:51:54.080
that Misener must have
done something wrong.

00:51:54.080 --> 00:51:58.720
He didn't. Both experiments
are true. How can both be true?

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:00.480
- You're measuring different things.

00:52:00.480 --> 00:52:04.080
I don't know, but how are you
measuring different things?

00:52:04.080 --> 00:52:06.880
This is where the second part
of Landau's work comes in.

00:52:06.880 --> 00:52:10.640
So far, Landau had only looked
at a fluid at absolute zero,

00:52:10.640 --> 00:52:14.080
but every real experiment
happened at higher temperatures.

00:52:14.080 --> 00:52:16.640
So next he considered what if it flows at

00:52:16.640 --> 00:52:18.640
some finite temperature?

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:22.000
To do this, he built on the
work of Tisza, who suggested

00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:24.480
that helium below 2.17 Kelvin acts

00:52:24.480 --> 00:52:26.960
as a mixture of two fluids.

00:52:26.960 --> 00:52:30.160
One is the superfluid. It's
effectively at absolute zero.

00:52:30.160 --> 00:52:33.040
It carries no heat and
flows without friction.

00:52:33.040 --> 00:52:36.080
You can think of this
as the background fluid.

00:52:36.080 --> 00:52:38.640
And the second is the normal
fluid, which is made up

00:52:38.640 --> 00:52:40.560
of the phonons and rotons.

00:52:40.560 --> 00:52:43.120
It carries all the heat and has viscosity.

00:52:43.120 --> 00:52:46.800
At absolute zero, the
whole fluid is superfluid,

00:52:46.800 --> 00:52:48.080
but as it heats up, part

00:52:48.080 --> 00:52:50.720
of the superfluid
turns into normal fluid.

00:52:50.720 --> 00:52:52.640
The superfluid fraction decreases

00:52:52.640 --> 00:52:56.880
and the normal fraction
increases until at 2.17 Kelvin,

00:52:56.880 --> 00:52:59.560
the superfluid component is zero.

00:52:59.560 --> 00:53:02.560
- Now, if you were to look
at one atom in the fluid

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:03.840
and say, "Is that superfluid

00:53:03.840 --> 00:53:06.320
or normal fluid?" Well, it's hard to tell

00:53:06.320 --> 00:53:09.760
because the superfluid atoms
are constantly exchanging

00:53:09.760 --> 00:53:11.510
energy with the normal fluid atoms.

00:53:11.510 --> 00:53:13.840
- Sure. - So any one atom
is constantly bouncing up

00:53:13.840 --> 00:53:15.680
and down between these two modes.

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:19.360
- Now, only the superfluid
can flow through tiny channels.

00:53:19.360 --> 00:53:21.360
So when Allen and Misener pushed helium

00:53:21.360 --> 00:53:23.840
through their capillaries,
they only measured the super

00:53:23.840 --> 00:53:26.720
fluid part and they
found a zero viscosity.

00:53:26.720 --> 00:53:29.200
But Misener's torsion experiment sat in a

00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:30.800
mixture of both fluids.

00:53:30.800 --> 00:53:34.480
So the cylinder felt the
viscosity from the normal fluid.

00:53:34.480 --> 00:53:36.320
Misener hadn't made a mistake,

00:53:36.320 --> 00:53:39.120
they were just measuring different things.

00:53:39.120 --> 00:53:40.720
And once you know what you're looking for,

00:53:40.720 --> 00:53:43.200
you can also physically see the effect.

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:46.720
Just below the transition
temperature, say at 2.15 Kelvin,

00:53:46.720 --> 00:53:49.920
only around 13% of the
helium is superfluid.

00:53:49.920 --> 00:53:52.000
So only that 13% can leak

00:53:52.000 --> 00:53:54.560
through the bottom and you get a drip.

00:53:54.560 --> 00:53:57.200
But if you cool it down
to 1.5 Kelvin, well now

00:53:57.200 --> 00:53:59.760
around 89% is superfluid.

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:02.320
That's almost seven times
as much helium that can flow

00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:06.320
through, so it just pours out the bottom.

00:54:06.320 --> 00:54:10.080
It even explains how light
can power a fountain.

00:54:10.080 --> 00:54:12.800
When the light hits the
rocks, the rocks absorb some

00:54:12.800 --> 00:54:14.640
of the photons and heat up a little

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:15.120
- Bit.

00:54:15.120 --> 00:54:16.960
- This causes part of the superfluid

00:54:16.960 --> 00:54:19.120
to turn into normal fluid,

00:54:19.120 --> 00:54:21.440
but now the superfluid density is lower

00:54:21.440 --> 00:54:24.880
inside the fountain, so more
superfluid immediately rushes

00:54:24.880 --> 00:54:27.600
in through the bottom to
restore the proportions.

00:54:27.600 --> 00:54:30.480
That bottom, however, is just
like the bottom of the bucket.

00:54:30.480 --> 00:54:32.560
It only allows superfluid through.

00:54:32.560 --> 00:54:35.280
So the normal fluid can't go out that way.

00:54:35.280 --> 00:54:39.280
The only way the excess
helium can go is up and out.

00:54:39.280 --> 00:54:41.040
And so you end up with this,

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:43.600
a fountain powered solely by light.

00:54:43.600 --> 00:54:46.080
The heat from the light gets
turned into the mechanical

00:54:46.080 --> 00:54:49.200
energy of the spouting helium.

00:54:49.200 --> 00:54:52.160
But the two fluids do
more than make fountains.

00:54:52.160 --> 00:54:55.040
They change how helium transfers heat.

00:54:55.040 --> 00:54:57.680
Take a bath of liquid helium
below the lambda point

00:54:57.680 --> 00:54:59.160
with a heater on one side.

00:54:59.160 --> 00:55:03.031
- The helium atoms that are in
contact with that hot face -

00:55:03.031 --> 00:55:04.320
- Yeah.
- Are going to gain energy. They're

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:05.750
going to jump up this ladder.

00:55:05.750 --> 00:55:07.200
- Yeah. - So they're going to
be part of the normal fluid.

00:55:07.200 --> 00:55:12.320
- Right.
- So my normal fluid is going

00:55:12.320 --> 00:55:15.520
to flow away from the heater,
carrying all of the heat

00:55:15.520 --> 00:55:17.990
and the entropy that's
imparted into the fluid away.

00:55:17.990 --> 00:55:21.200
- Yeah. - But as the normal
fluid flows away - Yeah.

00:55:21.200 --> 00:55:24.720
The superfluid flows
back to replace it. Right.

00:55:24.720 --> 00:55:28.160
And the overall density of
the fluid remains constant.

00:55:28.160 --> 00:55:31.120
- Sure.
- So this is a two-fluid

00:55:31.120 --> 00:55:32.960
thermomechanical counterflow.

00:55:32.960 --> 00:55:34.480
They basically flow through each other

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:35.680
like they weren't even there.

00:55:35.680 --> 00:55:38.080
- But you can take this one step further.

00:55:38.080 --> 00:55:39.360
Imagine changing the temperature

00:55:39.360 --> 00:55:41.280
of the heat source periodically.

00:55:41.280 --> 00:55:43.120
What is going to happen?

00:55:43.120 --> 00:55:45.440
Well, the amount of
normal fluid created will

00:55:45.440 --> 00:55:47.280
also change periodically.

00:55:47.280 --> 00:55:49.840
So you get these fluctuations
in the normal fluid

00:55:49.840 --> 00:55:51.600
and superfluid densities.

00:55:51.600 --> 00:55:54.080
They spread out and travel away,

00:55:54.080 --> 00:55:56.040
almost like a sort of sound.

00:55:56.040 --> 00:55:59.680
- When we think of first
sound, regular sound - Yeah.

00:55:59.680 --> 00:56:02.640
That's density waves in the air driven

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:04.560
by tiny pressure gradients.

00:56:04.560 --> 00:56:07.600
- But with a superfluid, you
can get density waves in the

00:56:07.600 --> 00:56:09.520
normal and superfluid components.

00:56:09.520 --> 00:56:11.840
And since the normal fluid
carries all the heat,

00:56:11.840 --> 00:56:13.840
that means you get temperature waves.

00:56:13.840 --> 00:56:16.311
- And they can spread
out like sound waves.

00:56:16.311 --> 00:56:17.360
- Yeah.
- So we call temperature waves

00:56:17.360 --> 00:56:19.080
in helium second sound.

00:56:19.080 --> 00:56:23.440
- The crazy thing about it
is it's a completely new mode

00:56:23.440 --> 00:56:27.760
of heat transport, unlike
anything else in the uni - right?

00:56:27.760 --> 00:56:29.200
Yeah. When I heard that, I was like,

00:56:29.200 --> 00:56:32.320
"That's crazy." So you
always think of conduction,

00:56:32.320 --> 00:56:34.400
convection, and radiation.

00:56:34.400 --> 00:56:37.280
And now helium gets a fourth.

00:56:37.280 --> 00:56:40.080
- This is a combination of second sound

00:56:40.080 --> 00:56:43.040
and thermal counterflow,
and it is effective,

00:56:43.040 --> 00:56:45.760
around a thousand times
more conductive than copper,

00:56:45.760 --> 00:56:48.320
which is one of the best
thermal conductors we know of.

00:56:48.320 --> 00:56:51.040
It finally helps explain one
last thing you've been seeing

00:56:51.040 --> 00:56:56.960
this whole time, that is why
does the boiling suddenly stop?

00:56:56.960 --> 00:56:58.960
Well, above 2.17 Kelvin,

00:56:58.960 --> 00:57:01.600
helium boils just like a normal liquid.

00:57:01.600 --> 00:57:05.920
- Say, for example, I've
got a bucket of water

00:57:05.920 --> 00:57:08.560
and I take a red hot
poker and I dump it in.

00:57:08.560 --> 00:57:10.560
The bubbles aren't going to
form anywhere in the bucket.

00:57:10.560 --> 00:57:12.880
They're going to form around that poker

00:57:12.880 --> 00:57:15.360
because you've got this
high temperature region

00:57:15.360 --> 00:57:19.040
where you've dumped in
enough energy locally

00:57:19.040 --> 00:57:20.960
to vaporize the water

00:57:20.960 --> 00:57:22.800
and then that's where the
bubbles are going to form.

00:57:22.800 --> 00:57:24.000
The amount of energy it takes

00:57:24.000 --> 00:57:26.880
to do this is the latent
heat of vaporization.

00:57:26.880 --> 00:57:29.920
In water, that is about

00:57:29.920 --> 00:57:33.440
2,200 joules per gram.

00:57:33.440 --> 00:57:37.680
In helium, it's about 23 joules per gram.

00:57:37.680 --> 00:57:42.240
So if you've got helium
in its liquid state,

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:45.760
a tiny thermal fluctuation is enough

00:57:45.760 --> 00:57:48.640
to locally vaporize a small pocket,

00:57:48.640 --> 00:57:49.840
which then forms a bubble.

00:57:49.840 --> 00:57:52.080
And that's why boiling is so constant,

00:57:52.080 --> 00:57:54.240
because tiny thermal
fluctuations will form

00:57:54.240 --> 00:57:56.120
bubbles very, very easily.

00:57:56.120 --> 00:58:00.480
- But as you cool helium
down below 2.17 kelvin,

00:58:00.480 --> 00:58:02.480
thermal counterflows take over.

00:58:02.480 --> 00:58:04.160
They transfer heat so effectively

00:58:04.160 --> 00:58:06.960
that the entire liquid almost immediately

00:58:06.960 --> 00:58:08.720
reaches the same temperature.

00:58:08.720 --> 00:58:10.000
So there are no hotspots

00:58:10.000 --> 00:58:12.640
and no places for bubbles to form,

00:58:12.640 --> 00:58:16.320
the boiling suddenly stops.

00:58:16.320 --> 00:58:17.840
This lack of boiling paired

00:58:17.840 --> 00:58:21.200
with its unparalleled heat
transfer also makes superfluid

00:58:21.200 --> 00:58:24.080
helium the ideal coolant
at one or two kelvin,

00:58:24.080 --> 00:58:27.520
and that's why CERN uses
around 700,000 liters of it

00:58:27.520 --> 00:58:31.600
to cool the LHC's 9,000
superconducting magnets.

00:58:31.600 --> 00:58:37.040
- There is still one thing
that bothers me though, Chris,

00:58:37.040 --> 00:58:40.080
which is that, you know,
if you take any substance

00:58:40.080 --> 00:58:44.160
and you keep cooling it
down, it always freezes.

00:58:44.160 --> 00:58:47.440
But helium never does, yet

00:58:47.440 --> 00:58:50.000
it does liquefy, which
is the annoying part.

00:58:50.000 --> 00:58:51.760
So because it liquefies, it means

00:58:51.760 --> 00:58:54.560
that there is some
attraction between the atoms.

00:58:54.560 --> 00:58:55.680
And so you'd expect, you know,

00:58:55.680 --> 00:58:57.760
I can just keep cooling
it down more and more

00:58:57.760 --> 00:59:01.120
and more until eventually
the kinetic energy becomes

00:59:01.120 --> 00:59:02.880
arbitrarily small

00:59:02.880 --> 00:59:05.840
and it must, like the attractive
energy must be much larger

00:59:05.840 --> 00:59:07.920
and so it must form a solid.

00:59:07.920 --> 00:59:11.440
- Yes. So this is the kind of

00:59:11.440 --> 00:59:13.360
intuitive picture that you would expect.

00:59:13.360 --> 00:59:17.280
Well, you can't answer
that in an intuitive way.

00:59:17.280 --> 00:59:19.280
You have to bring in quantum mechanics.

00:59:19.280 --> 00:59:21.360
- Because according to quantum mechanics,

00:59:21.360 --> 00:59:24.000
the more precisely you
know a particle's position,

00:59:24.000 --> 00:59:27.040
the less you know about its
momentum and vice versa.

00:59:27.040 --> 00:59:29.520
This is known as Heisenberg's
uncertainty principle,

00:59:29.520 --> 00:59:31.200
and mathematically it tells you

00:59:31.200 --> 00:59:33.840
that the uncertainty in
position delta x times the

00:59:33.840 --> 00:59:36.560
uncertainty in momentum
delta p is greater than

00:59:36.560 --> 00:59:38.800
or equal to h-bar over two.

00:59:38.800 --> 00:59:41.920
For simplicity, let's just
say it's around h-bar.

00:59:41.920 --> 00:59:45.200
- Let's say I've got an atom in a liquid

00:59:45.200 --> 00:59:46.960
and it has some freedom.

00:59:46.960 --> 00:59:48.080
It can move around,

00:59:48.080 --> 00:59:50.160
but you know, it's very closely packs in

00:59:50.160 --> 00:59:51.920
with all of its neighboring atoms.

00:59:51.920 --> 00:59:55.280
Let's say that it occupies
some spherical volume

00:59:55.280 --> 00:59:57.600
with a radius r.

00:59:57.600 --> 00:59:59.760
Well, we can say that that r

00:59:59.760 --> 01:00:02.640
is The uncertainty in its position.

01:00:02.640 --> 01:00:06.640
So if the uncertainty in the
momentum is going to look like

01:00:06.640 --> 01:00:10.880
h-bar, planks constant, over R.

01:00:10.880 --> 01:00:13.120
- But if it's got an
uncertainty in momentum,

01:00:13.120 --> 01:00:16.080
it means it must be jiggling
around inside that sphere.

01:00:16.080 --> 01:00:20.000
- We know if it's jiggling,
we can say it's got an energy.

01:00:20.000 --> 01:00:21.560
Well, we know what it's momentum is.

01:00:21.560 --> 01:00:25.920
It's planks constant over, over R.

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:27.680
So how do we get the
energy? Well, it's easy.

01:00:27.680 --> 01:00:31.920
We say it's the momentum
squared over twice the mass.

01:00:31.920 --> 01:00:35.520
Now, helium being such a light atom,

01:00:35.520 --> 01:00:38.160
that mass turn is very small.

01:00:38.160 --> 01:00:40.560
So that means that the energy that it has,

01:00:40.560 --> 01:00:44.720
because it's confined,
is very, very large.

01:00:44.720 --> 01:00:47.040
Now, this is what we call
the zero point energy.

01:00:47.040 --> 01:00:49.360
This is the energy it cannot get rid of.

01:00:49.360 --> 01:00:51.360
It's nothing to do with thermal energy.

01:00:51.360 --> 01:00:53.680
It's not because there's a
temperature in the liquid.

01:00:53.680 --> 01:00:56.800
In fact, when you're in the liquid state,

01:00:56.800 --> 01:01:00.240
that zero point energy
accounts for more than 98%

01:01:00.240 --> 01:01:02.280
of the total energy in the fluid.

01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:05.200
- Wait, what?
- It is, it is enormous.

01:01:05.200 --> 01:01:07.440
- That is crazy. I had no clue.

01:01:07.440 --> 01:01:11.440
- This zero point energy is
too large for the atoms ever

01:01:11.440 --> 01:01:13.200
to lock down in place.

01:01:13.200 --> 01:01:16.920
So helium never freezes
just by cooling it down.

01:01:16.920 --> 01:01:20.400
- So when you're thinking of
this kind of ladder of states,

01:01:20.400 --> 01:01:22.400
this kind of BEC picture, like, you know,

01:01:22.400 --> 01:01:25.440
everything's macroscopically
occupying this bottom state,

01:01:25.440 --> 01:01:28.320
we think we, the, the
intuition is to think of it

01:01:28.320 --> 01:01:33.040
as like ladder where each rung
is nice and evenly spaced.

01:01:33.040 --> 01:01:34.400
But the way to think of it in terms

01:01:34.400 --> 01:01:35.840
of this zero point energy is that

01:01:35.840 --> 01:01:40.080
that first rung is 10
stories off the floor.

01:01:40.080 --> 01:01:42.960
And then the thermal states
stack up on top of that.

01:01:42.960 --> 01:01:46.880
- Oh, that's so good.
- But there is one way in which you

01:01:46.880 --> 01:01:50.160
can freeze helium, which was
discovered by Onnes's successor.

01:01:50.160 --> 01:01:52.960
That is by cooling helium
down to about one Kelvin

01:01:52.960 --> 01:01:56.320
and by increasing the
pressure to 25 atmospheres.

01:01:56.320 --> 01:01:59.360
If you do that, helium finally freezes.

01:01:59.360 --> 01:02:04.160
As for Onnes, he died in
1926 at the age of 72.

01:02:04.160 --> 01:02:07.920
He had been making super
fluid helium for 18 years,

01:02:07.920 --> 01:02:10.320
looking at it, seeing hint after hint,

01:02:10.320 --> 01:02:11.760
and yet he never realized

01:02:11.760 --> 01:02:14.720
what he'd been staring at the entire time.

01:02:14.720 --> 01:02:15.840
Now, you might look at this

01:02:15.840 --> 01:02:19.280
and think HOnnes was a little
stupid or ignorant to miss it

01:02:19.280 --> 01:02:21.120
and to not put the pieces together,

01:02:21.120 --> 01:02:22.720
but I don't think that's the case.

01:02:22.720 --> 01:02:25.040
Instead, I think it reveals a fundamental

01:02:25.040 --> 01:02:26.960
feature of being human.

01:02:26.960 --> 01:02:28.640
I think it goes to show a lot

01:02:28.640 --> 01:02:31.200
of people think you got
to see it to believe it.

01:02:31.200 --> 01:02:35.440
And I think often you have to
believe it in order to see it.

01:02:35.440 --> 01:02:39.760
It makes me think a lot
about Kuhns, the structure

01:02:39.760 --> 01:02:41.280
of scientific revolutions,

01:02:41.280 --> 01:02:43.840
because it focuses a lot
on, you know, you have the

01:02:43.840 --> 01:02:45.760
existing science, you
have the existing paradigms,

01:02:45.760 --> 01:02:46.880
you have the existing formulas

01:02:46.880 --> 01:02:49.440
that these things are supposed to follow.

01:02:49.440 --> 01:02:52.000
And how often would the

01:02:52.000 --> 01:02:54.880
data point going off the
line just be some error,

01:02:54.880 --> 01:02:58.000
someone messed up, condensation,
something happening?

01:02:58.000 --> 01:03:00.000
It's easy to write those things off.

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:01.840
So I have a lot of sympathy

01:03:01.840 --> 01:03:04.400
and I don't think, "Oh, how
could they miss it?" I kind

01:03:04.400 --> 01:03:05.920
of think like, "No, that's actually

01:03:05.920 --> 01:03:08.560
how we operate." Especially, I

01:03:08.560 --> 01:03:09.760
don't know if scientists are supposed

01:03:09.760 --> 01:03:11.040
to be a little bit more observant

01:03:11.040 --> 01:03:12.160
or a little bit more skeptical,

01:03:12.160 --> 01:03:14.960
but at the same time, you're up against

01:03:14.960 --> 01:03:18.240
so much chaos in the
world that you do kind

01:03:18.240 --> 01:03:22.480
of sometimes just see,
"Yeah, yeah, that fits that.

01:03:22.480 --> 01:03:26.960
Yeah, that fits the data."
And they sort of ignore things

01:03:26.960 --> 01:03:28.160
that don't fit because you're looking

01:03:28.160 --> 01:03:29.680
for something. I don't know.

01:03:29.680 --> 01:03:33.120
- Yeah. But I think probably
especially if you are a

01:03:33.120 --> 01:03:36.480
scientist, it's these unexpected things

01:03:36.480 --> 01:03:38.880
that can end up having
the biggest breakthrough

01:03:38.880 --> 01:03:42.400
because those are the
things that disconfirm

01:03:42.400 --> 01:03:43.600
your view of reality.

01:03:43.600 --> 01:03:46.400
- And the thing is, often all the signs

01:03:46.400 --> 01:03:48.000
for the breakthrough are there.

01:03:48.000 --> 01:03:50.240
It's just that we're
not ready to see them.

01:03:50.240 --> 01:03:54.640
So it makes you wonder what
signs are we missing right now?

01:03:54.640 --> 01:03:57.040
I used to think that the
reason someone didn't make the

01:03:57.040 --> 01:03:59.280
breakthrough was because maybe
they weren't smart enough

01:03:59.280 --> 01:04:02.480
or they didn't work hard enough
or didn't get lucky enough.

01:04:02.480 --> 01:04:05.200
But now I feel like I've
always missed something.

01:04:05.200 --> 01:04:07.840
Just look at Onnes. He
had all of those things.

01:04:07.840 --> 01:04:10.880
He was an experimental genius,
had an immense work ethic

01:04:10.880 --> 01:04:13.200
and even discovered
super connectivity, one

01:04:13.200 --> 01:04:15.920
of the biggest breakthroughs
of the 20th century.

01:04:15.920 --> 01:04:17.920
He got three out of three.

01:04:17.920 --> 01:04:21.440
So perhaps sometimes making the
breakthrough doesn't require

01:04:21.440 --> 01:04:24.720
any extra intelligence,
hard work or even luck.

01:04:24.720 --> 01:04:27.520
Maybe all it takes is for us to let go

01:04:27.520 --> 01:04:29.680
of the way we believe the world to be

01:04:29.680 --> 01:04:31.840
and instead, just for a moment, try

01:04:31.840 --> 01:04:34.000
to see it as it really is.

01:04:34.000 --> 01:04:36.400
What if that is all it takes?

01:04:36.400 --> 01:04:39.840
And Onnes, well, he may
have missed superfluidity,

01:04:39.840 --> 01:04:42.560
but he changed cold
temperature physics forever.

01:04:42.560 --> 01:04:45.440
And in 1913, he received
the Nobel Prize in physics

01:04:45.440 --> 01:04:47.600
for his achievements at finding out

01:04:47.600 --> 01:04:52.240
what happens when you
just keep cooling down.

01:04:54.320 --> 01:04:56.080
Hey, two quick final things.

01:04:56.080 --> 01:04:58.240
First, this video would not
have been possible without the

01:04:58.240 --> 01:05:00.880
help of Professor Weinfurtner's
Gravity Laboratory at

01:05:00.880 --> 01:05:02.800
Nottingham University and the funding

01:05:02.800 --> 01:05:05.600
provided to them to run their
superfluid experiments.

01:05:05.600 --> 01:05:07.840
So I want to give a big
thank you to all of them

01:05:07.840 --> 01:05:10.960
for helping us recreate these experiments.

01:05:10.960 --> 01:05:13.200
The second thing is
that we didn't even get

01:05:13.200 --> 01:05:14.720
to show you their research

01:05:14.720 --> 01:05:17.200
because while it might seem
like we've solved all the

01:05:17.200 --> 01:05:18.480
mysteries of superfluids,

01:05:18.480 --> 01:05:20.880
it turns out we're just getting started

01:05:20.880 --> 01:05:23.200
because really weird things happen when

01:05:23.200 --> 01:05:24.800
you start rotating them.

01:05:24.800 --> 01:05:25.920
And this rotation

01:05:25.920 --> 01:05:28.720
or the physics of it can play
a role in everything from

01:05:28.720 --> 01:05:31.840
neutron stars to simulating
rotating black holes

01:05:31.840 --> 01:05:34.080
to a black hole bomb.

01:05:34.080 --> 01:05:36.720
And because if you look
closely enough, you can kind

01:05:36.720 --> 01:05:40.080
of see the entire universe
in a drop of superfluid.

01:05:40.080 --> 01:05:48.800
So let us know if you want us
to make a part two on that.

